>	After reading about (and seeing posters about) the proposed
>telecommunications policy proposed here at GMU, I am appalled.  

I didn't see the original policy posted here; might you be able to
post it here?

>I do agree that the system does get bogged down.  However, isn't a 
>simple solution to this problem simply to add more memory to the
>mainframes?  

Not necessarily.  Heavy demand could create a need for anything from
additional RAM (as you mention) to additional disk space, expanded
network equipment or even a complete rewiring job.  What changes have
been suggestioned/mentioned?

>	GMU is supposed to be composed of an academic community.  
>Why should I have part of my intellectual freedom (I do consider
>doing whatever I want on the internet to be part of my intellectual
>freedom) taken away from me?  

You may wish to rephrase this sentence.  I can think of *many* things,
possible (and even easy) on the Internet, that aren't really covered by 
'intellectual freedom.'  Neither intellectual freedom nor its cousin, 
academic freedom, confer the _carte blanche_ of "whatever I want."

Perhaps the more important question for you is this - given the fact
that computational/network resources are finite, is there a point at
which your exercise of 'intellectual freedom' prevents others from 
exercising theirs?  If you answer affirmatively, you have to lose the
'whatever I want' attitude; it just doen't work.

--Wes

