Enforcement works through terror, not mass arrests.  They just
need to arrest one single provider to strike fear into the 
hearts of all of the others.  The providers who decide to remain
in the game then have to set down all kinds of rules and make
it look like they are seriously trying to keep "bad things" from
going across their wires.

The small providers, those who don't have a legal staff and can't
do a lot of monitoring, are essentially eliminated.  So we end
up with Microsoft, AOL and whoever else is still providing access.

This bill could drive out Freenets, would make employers very
uptight about any employee use of the Net, etc.  Note that the
"provider" is responsible for the content, not the law 
enforcement agencies.  And the provider takes on the liability.

Also note that no one has ever arrested the phone company for 
the content going over their wires, though I can guarantee you
that it's a rare crime that doesn't involve some use of the
telephone.

