>It merely said (to me, anyway) that parties could file complaints with
>the appropriate office.  Assuming that this procedure follows due pro-
>cess, I fail to see a problem.  

Such a statement where the bounds of actionable harassment are drawn
*very* unclearly suggests that the administration is soliciting complaints
over even clearly protected expression.  I certainly can't conclude that
the administration's position is that it solicits complaints that on matters
it does not intend to act upon.


>I would certainly hope that facetious or baseless complaints would be
>rejected out of hand, but we'll have to wait and see.  In and of it-
>self, this message seemed to direct potential problems into the UCSD
>due process system - where they belong.

Even if the university *does* intend to respond to complaints in accordance
with the First Amendment and California law, soliciting complaints with
a statement this vague has a very good chance of having a significant
chilling effect upon expression.  In essence, the statement solicits
"nuissance" complaints.  Some individuals might well choose to just shut
up, rather than say anything even remotely controversial, for fear of
having to respond to (even baseless, nuissance) complaints.

The university should feel free to condemn (from an ethical standpoint)
certain types of expression, but it ought to be much clearer about what
constitutes *actionable* harassment, in order to avoid these problems as
best as possible.


>Hey, he very well could have announced a 'dirty word filter' that would
>start policing email, right?  Let's not tar them without reason.

The fact that a bad policy could have been worse doesn't usually make me
