Although not a religious person, I always believed that 666 was an
evil number: my own political party proved it when they unveiled
their House Resolution to allow searches and siezures without
warrants.

HR666 wipes the Exclusionary Rule and the Fourth Amendment off the
slate. As an American citizen, I would like to think that our
freedom is etched in stone, unable to be removed. However, the GOP
would like to take an electric sander and make a good attempt to
make these two laws illegible.

I give credit to "John4norml" for pointing out that this Resolution
leads to police abuse. However, I disagree with him on one point.
Most people who are stopped on highways by troopers allow for their
cars to be searched, unless the officer sees something in plain sight,
or has reasonable suspicion of possession of narcotics or alcohol.

The problem with police abuse stems from the phrase within the bill:
JUST CAUSE. HR666 allows for searches and siezures to be performed
with just cause. The problem is that anything can be labelled "just
cause." 

I can be typing e-mail here on my computer, and a police officer peaks
into my window and sees me typing, he can break into my home claiming
that he had just cause to do so because he thought that I may have been 
making a drug deal over the internet. 

I can be driving on Intertate 81, and talking to my girlfriend on my
car phone, and a state trooper can pull me over and search my car for
evidence of conspiring to assassinate Bill Clinton because he had
"just cause" to do so.

I can be home alone, playing Snoop Doggy Dogg on my stereo loud enough
for people in Japan to hear it. And a police officer breaks down my
door and searches my house for marijuana because he had "just cause" to
do so due to the fact that I was listening to rap music (which had 
lyrics about smoking marijuana).

Although these examples may seem extreme, they are here to illustrate
the outcome of HR666 -- excessive police abuse. Impacts of this are terrible:
1. Less criminals will be actually caught. This type of abusive action
will take police officers away from the actual criminals when there are
no reports of crime (this will most likely happen in rural and suburbian
areas of our nation).
2. Less trust in our police force. The media loves to pick up on negative
things. It will only take about three to five reports of police abuse to 
have American citizens trust police officers less. This leads to my third
impact -- 
3. First step to a police state. Not only will citizens trust the police
less, but they will fear the police more. Fear of the police and having the pol
police exercise force is the first step to a police force. 

The GOP claims to prevent this by not allowing the resolution apply to the
IRS or the ATF, but that's just a small sector of the police force in the 
US. Overall, the negative effects of HR666 far outweigh any good that
could come out of this bill.

Paul Swiderski
prswider@rs01.kings.edu


